# CI/CD UAT Proof of Concept

A minimal Node/Express API that proves out a full CI/CD pipeline: push code →
automatically test → automatically deploy to a UAT server.

## What this proves

If this pipeline works, it demonstrates that your team can trust GitHub
Actions to automatically build, test, and deploy code to UAT without a
manual step — the same pattern can then be extended to your real projects.

## Project structure

```
nodejs-cicd-poc/
├── server.js                        # Express API (2 endpoints: /health, /api/status)
├── test.js                          # Smoke test run by CI before deploy
├── package.json
└── .github/workflows/ci-cd-uat.yml  # The actual CI/CD pipeline definition
```

## One-time setup

### 1. Prepare the UAT server (Linux, SSH access assumed)

SSH into your UAT server and confirm Node.js and pm2 are installed:

```bash
node -v        # should be 18+
npm install -g pm2
```

Create the app folder the pipeline will deploy into:

```bash
mkdir -p ~/apps/cicd-uat-poc
```

Open the port you plan to run on (default 3000) in your firewall/security
group if it isn't already.

### 2. Generate an SSH key for GitHub Actions to use

On your own machine (not the UAT server):

```bash
ssh-keygen -t ed25519 -C "github-actions-deploy" -f ./uat_deploy_key
```

This creates two files: `uat_deploy_key` (private) and `uat_deploy_key.pub`
(public).

Copy the **public** key to the UAT server's authorized keys:

```bash
ssh-copy-id -i uat_deploy_key.pub youruser@your-uat-host
```

### 3. Add GitHub repo secrets

In your GitHub repo: **Settings → Secrets and variables → Actions → New
repository secret**. Add these four:

| Secret name | Value |
|---|---|
| `UAT_HOST` | Your UAT server's IP or hostname |
| `UAT_USERNAME` | SSH username on the UAT server |
| `UAT_SSH_KEY` | Full contents of the **private** key file (`uat_deploy_key`) |
| `UAT_PORT` | SSH port, usually `22` |

### 4. Push to trigger it

The pipeline runs automatically on every push to the `uat` branch:

```bash
git checkout -b uat
git add .
git commit -m "Initial CI/CD UAT POC"
git push origin uat
```

Watch it run under the **Actions** tab in your GitHub repo.

### 5. Verify the deployment worked

Once the pipeline finishes, hit the UAT server directly:

```bash
curl http://your-uat-host:3000/api/status
```

You should see the current git commit SHA in the `version` field — proof
that the exact code you pushed is what's now running on UAT.

## Extending this POC

Once this works end-to-end, the same pattern extends to a real project:

- Swap `server.js` for your actual app
- Add a real test suite (Jest, Mocha) in place of the smoke test
- Add a build step if using TypeScript or a frontend bundler
- Add a separate workflow file for production, gated behind manual approval
- If your UAT server is Windows/IIS instead of Linux, replace the
  scp/ssh-action steps with a Windows-compatible deploy method (e.g.
  `web-deploy` / `msdeploy`, or a self-hosted Windows GitHub Actions
  runner installed directly on the UAT box)